# How I work

## Style
- Never use em dashes. Not in prose, not in commit messages, not in notes.
- Plain declarative sentences. Professional but not formal. Don't overstate or oversell.
- No "What we found" / "What we did" headings. No bullets inside email bodies.
- Say what a thing is, then stop. Don't pad with detail I didn't ask for.
- My vault and repo prose is mostly Claude-written. Never quote it back to me as my own
  words, and never use it as a source for a research question. Go to outside sources.

## Asking and proposing
- Always ask before saving a file, and ask where it should go. Never pick a location yourself.
- When an action would write to Todoist, Gmail, Calendar, or GitHub: propose one item, then
  stop. Not one batch. One item.
- "Confirm X" means check whether X is already true. It is never a new requirement.
- Act only at full certainty. Otherwise say what's uncertain and ask.
- Needing clarification: use the question tool, up to four at once. In chat, a numbered list.

## Delivering
- Complete scripts and files, never fragments or snippets.
- Email replies, queries and short answers go in chat. Not an artifact, not a file.
- Write-ups, drafts and extracted data are deliverables, not commits. Don't put them in a repo.
- For anything on a remote host, hand me one copy-pasteable command and wait for the output.
- UI work: show labelled mockups first and let me pick. Don't implement until I do.
- Artifacts use the ayu light palette and semantic light/dark CSS token pairs.

## Git
- Never commit unless I asked in that message. "Create a branch" is not permission to commit.
- Never add a Co-Authored-By or any attribution trailer unless the repo's own file says to.
- Default to a branch off main. A worktree only if I ask, and clean it up after.
- One consolidated PR per unit of work. Don't split it.
- Never run `git push --force`, `git gc`, `git repack` or `git prune` in a repo under
  ~/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox. An object store there has already been destroyed once.

## Done means
- Targeted tests first. Full suite, lint and type-check before you call it done.
- Coverage is welded into addopts in hurin, rivendell and media-organizer, so a subset run
  needs `--no-cov` or it fails with every test passing. Run the full `make test` before pushing.
- If a repo has no mypy config, don't add one yourself. Show me the options (how strict, which
  paths, whether CI gates on it, the error count at each level) and ask which I want. Same for
  ruff, a formatter, or a coverage floor. Never add tooling config unasked.
- Check the work, don't just do it: read back every write to a file or service, and cite the
  command or output that proves it's done. A live check that sends, posts or writes is still
  proposed as one item first.

## Local tools
- Nothing is on PATH in the Python repos. Everything runs through `uv run`.
- Financial queries go through `monarch --read-only --json` (`--from`/`--to` for a range).
